<!--
    Type campaign, item charbyte: a byte loaded through a char pointer is
    declared unsigned char, and so is the pointer.  coreutils fmt -O2 get_line
    shows it on a string walk:

        v1 = (unsigned char *)*dat_c100;
        v7 = strchr("([\'`\"",(int)(char)*v1);

    and every gnulib mbrtowc wrapper takes unsigned char *a1 and passes
    (char *)a1 to mbrtowc.

    Root cause (P5, type propagation).  x86 loads a byte with movzx whether
    the program meant it signed or not, so *p is a LOAD followed by INT_ZEXT.
    TypeOpIntZext::getInputLocal votes getBase(1, TYPE_UINT) for the byte, and
    in the getLocalType fold SUB_UINT_PLAIN (16) outranks SUB_INT_CHAR (19), so
    the byte is seeded uint1.  The char * that reaches the LOAD's address
    cannot displace it, and the byte then pushes uint1 * back over the pointer.

    Stage / sub-stage: P5 "type-propagation".  The P0 assertion
    option charbyte on|off decides whether such a byte is re-seeded char.

    The fixture, SysV x86-64:

        isdash (char *s, uint4 *out):   movzx eax,[rdi]; mov [rsi],eax;
                                        cmp al,0x2d; sete al
            the witness: the byte is widened into *out and compared with '-'
        isdashu (uint1 *t, uint4 *out): the same bytes
            a control: a byte read through an unsigned char pointer stays
            uint1 and its constant a number in both passes
        highbit (char *s, uint4 *out):  cmp al,0x7f; seta al
            a control: the byte is also compared UNSIGNED, which is the
            program treating it as a number, so it stays uint1
        ishigh (char *s, uint4 *out):   cmp al,0xe9; sete al
            the correctness pin: a constant at or above 0x80 must take char
            WITH the byte.  A char compared with 0xe9 is never equal in C, so
            the rule re-seeds the byte and propagates again rather than
            overriding one edge, and the constant prints as the char it is
        dashlen (no prototype):         strlen(s); movzx edx,[s];
                                        mov [out],edx; cmp dl,0x2d
            the pointer is char * only because strlen takes one; with the
            option off the byte pushes uint1 * over it and the call needs a
            (char *) cast
        strlen: xor eax,eax; ret
        argbyte (no prototype):         strlen(s); movzx ebx,[s];
                                        mov esi,ebx; call logit;
                                        return n + byte
            a control: the byte reaches the call with its movzx folded
            into a one-byte argument, where C would sign-extend a char
            (logit("%d",c) would pass -128 for 0x80, the binary 128), so
            it keeps uint1 in both passes
        sibling (no prototype):         strlen(s); movzx ebp,[s];
                                        movzx esi,[s+1]; call logit
            a control: s[0] qualifies, but s[1], read through the same
            pointer, is passed to the call; retyping the pointer would make
            it a char argument, so the function keeps uint1 * in both passes
        counter (gcc -O0):              c = *p; *p = c + 1; return c * 4
            a control: the only char evidence is the default signed vote of
            the byte's own sum, stored back through the pointer; a counter
            keeps uint1 in both passes
        logit: ret
        highcase (no prototype):        strlen(s); movzx edx,[s];
                                        jmp [(edx-0x7e)*8 + table]
            a control: the byte selects a switch whose labels run past
            0x7f; a char switch variable never equals case 0x80, so it
            keeps uint1 in both passes

    Pass 1 (option charbyte off) reproduces the bug; pass 2 (on) is the fix.
-->
